WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:03.461
I'm a behaviorist by training,
so I'm interested

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:06.464
in understanding the evolution
of behavior.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.051
And owl monkeys
are my expertise.

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:15.265 line:76%
They are found from the forest,
the tropical forests of Panama

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:19.728 line:76%
in Central America, down to the
northern portion of Argentina.

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:22.355
In every country
except The Guianas.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:26.776
The only volume on owl monkeys
was done 30 years ago,

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:28.445
1994 it was published.

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.613
And so I knew that at some point

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:32.365
I wanted to bring all of our
research

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:35.034
on owl monkeys in a synthesis,
in an edited volume.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.746
And in fact,
most of the 60 authors in

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:40.790
the book are from countries
where the owl monkeys range.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.543
Owl monkeys are the only

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.504
primates in the Americas
that have nocturnal habits.

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:48.798
The owl monkey lives in small
groups,

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:51.092
what we call monogamous
groups.

00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:53.595
The male and the female
establish a pair bond.

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.513
They only reproduce
with each other.

00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:57.056
And when the infants are born,

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:58.057
the father

00:00:58.057 --> 00:01:01.102
is one of the most amazing
fathers in the living world.

